---
import Docs from "../../layouts/Docs.astro";
import {
  MessageResponseFile,
  MessageResponseHandoff,
  MessageResponseLinks,
  MessageResponseMarkdown,
  MessageResponsePlayground,
  MessageResponseStreaming,
  MessageResponseTables,
  MessageResponseTaskHistory,
} from "../../components/MessageResponseDemo";
import CodeBlock from "../../components/CodeBlock.astro";
---

<Docs
  title="Message Response"
  description="Renders an assistant's Markdown reply: headings, lists, task lists, tables, code blocks, and links you can replace."
>
  <h1 class="docs-title">Message Response</h1>
  <p class="docs-lead">
    Turns the Markdown an agent writes into a readable reply. It supports GitHub
    tables and task lists, shows fenced code in a Code Block, drops raw HTML and
    images, and lets you replace links with citations or downloads.
  </p>
  <div class="mt-8"><MessageResponsePlayground client:load /></div>
  <section class="prose-section">
    <h2 class="section-title">Usage</h2>
    <CodeBlock
      lang="tsx"
      title="An assistant reply"
      code={`import { MessageResponse } from "@tulipfarm/sprout/message-response";

<MessageResponse streaming={message.status === "streaming"}>
  {message.text}
</MessageResponse>`}
    />
    <p>
      <code>children</code> is the Markdown string. There are no variants or sizes:
      the reply uses the body text size and fills its column. Long words and URLs
      wrap, so it never pushes a chat column wider.
    </p>
  </section>
  <section class="prose-section">
    <h2 class="section-title">Markdown</h2>
    <p>
      Headings, emphasis, strikethrough, nested and numbered lists, task lists,
      quotes, rules, aligned tables, autolinks, and fenced code. A fence with a
      language gets syntax colors; a fence without one shows as
      <code>text</code>. Raw HTML is dropped and images are not shown.
    </p>
    <div class="mt-6"><MessageResponseMarkdown client:load /></div>
  </section>
  <section class="prose-section">
    <h2 class="section-title">Streaming</h2>
    <p>
      Set <code>streaming</code> while tokens arrive. A pulsing caret follows the
      text and the reply is marked busy. An empty reply that is streaming shows only
      the caret, which is the waiting state.
    </p>
    <div class="mt-6"><MessageResponseStreaming client:load /></div>
  </section>
  <section class="prose-section">
    <h2 class="section-title">Table viewport</h2>
    <CodeBlock
      lang="tsx"
      title="Cap tables when the reply shows row cards"
      code={`import { MessageTableViewport } from "@tulipfarm/sprout/message-response";

<MessageTableViewport bounded={rowViews.length > 0}>
  <MessageResponse>{text}</MessageResponse>
</MessageTableViewport>`}
    />
    <p>
      Every table scrolls in its own focusable region. Inside a
      <code>bounded</code> viewport a table stops at 12rem and scrolls both ways with
      a sticky header, so row cards below it stay in view. Without one, tables grow
      to their full height and scroll sideways only when their columns cannot fit.
    </p>
    <div class="mt-6"><MessageResponseTables client:load /></div>
  </section>
  <section class="prose-section">
    <h2 class="section-title">Custom links</h2>
    <CodeBlock
      lang="tsx"
      title="Citations and workspace downloads"
      code={`<MessageResponse
  renderLink={(href, label) => {
    const citation = citationId(href);
    if (citation) return <Citation id={citation}>{label}</Citation>;
    if (isWorkspaceDownload(href)) return <DownloadLink path={href}>{label}</DownloadLink>;
    return <a href={href} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">{label}</a>;
  }}
>
  {text}
</MessageResponse>`}
    />
    <p>
      Without <code>renderLink</code>, links open in a new tab with
      <code>noopener</code>. With it, every link goes through your function, so
      return a plain link for the ones you do not handle. The examples use
      sample adapters: citations check access, then show the passage, a hidden
      notice, or a retry; downloads report progress, success, or failure.
    </p>
    <div class="mt-6"><MessageResponseLinks client:load /></div>
    <div class="mt-6"><MessageResponseTaskHistory client:load /></div>
  </section>
  <section class="prose-section">
    <h2 class="section-title">Markdown files</h2>
    <CodeBlock
      lang="tsx"
      title="Format Markdown in the file viewer"
      code={`<FileRenderersProvider
  renderers={{ markdown: ({ text }) => <MessageResponse>{text}</MessageResponse> }}
>
  <FileViewer file={file} open={open} onOpenChange={setOpen} />
</FileRenderersProvider>`}
    />
    <div class="mt-6"><MessageResponseFile client:load /></div>
  </section>
  <section class="prose-section">
    <h2 class="section-title">In a panel</h2>
    <p>
      Put a reply in a bordered panel when it sits among other details, such as
      a chat handoff.
    </p>
    <div class="mt-6"><MessageResponseHandoff client:load /></div>
  </section>
  <section class="prose-section">
    <h2 class="section-title">Accessibility</h2>
    <p>
      Headings, lists, quotes, and tables keep their native roles. Each table
      scroll region is named “Response table” and takes focus, so keyboard users
      can scroll it with the arrow keys. The reply sets
      <code>aria-busy</code> while it streams and the caret is hidden from screen
      readers. Links and table regions show a focus ring. The caret stops pulsing
      with reduced motion and uses the system text color in forced colors mode.
    </p>
  </section>
</Docs>
